#!/usr/bin/env node
// mem-serve — the dashboard at one fixed link.
//
// `mem viewer` writes a ONE-OFF HTML file: a photograph, thrown away
// when it goes stale. That is deliberately not a server. This is the
// other mode: one link that always shows the current state, and the
// only place in this project where anything can be SET without a
// shell.
//
// **Since 2026-09-28 the dashboard is the ONLY UI** (owner decision:
// the sibling house's Dashboard-Muster-3, ported — src/dashboard-page.mjs,
// src/dashboard-data.mjs, assets/dashboard/). `/` and `/dashboard`
// serve the same page; `/pult` (the sibling house's German word for it)
// is a redirect to `/dashboard` since 2026-10-01, kept only so old
// bookmarks still land. The old Astra desk, the console page and
// the viewer route are gone — switched off only after
// test/dashboard-complete.test.mjs proved every feature has a place in
// the dashboard. `/console` and `/viewer` answer with a redirect into
// the dashboard so bookmarks still land; the data routes (`/console.json`,
// `/desk.json`, `/entry.json`, `/entries.json`, `/task.json`) stay, and
// `/pult.json` redirects to `/desk.json` for the same reason `/pult` does.
//
// The trade is worth naming out loud. The viewer file was "no daemon,
// nothing that keeps running". This IS a daemon. So it carries the two
// properties the file had, and one more, because a link is reachable
// from outside:
//
//  1. It reads only through `dashboard-data.collectDashboard()` and
//     `console.collect()`, which read the redacted drawers — never the
//     bytes under `raw/`. No un-redacted capture material leaves the process.
//  2. It never writes the rendered content to disk, commits nothing,
//     publishes nothing. The HTML lives in RAM and goes to the browser
//     that asked.
//  3. Fail-closed on auth: with no CHEAP_MEM_SERVE_TOKEN it serves
//     localhost ONLY. Binding to a public address without a token is
//     REFUSED — the process does not start.
//
// The intended shape: bind to 127.0.0.1, put a tunnel or reverse proxy
// in front, and let that (or the token) be the door.

